// ==== src/paint/wipe.js
// paint/wipe.js : the rag on the paint layer (lane K-WIPE, 2026-09-05).  PK.smear is the wet pull that drags colour and lays none of its
// own; the wipe is its removing sibling: a rag or squeegee pass that THINS the film, carries the pigment it lifts along the pull and lays it
// down again as a smear that thins to nothing, leaves the ground (or the layer under) showing through in streaks, with one hard edge on
// the tool's side and a ragged edge where the rag lifted.  Rita Ackermann's later pictures live on it: a figure in dark line, then chalky
// pink or white dragged across half of it so it half disappears, then the line drawn again a little off (the hand word h.ghost, hand/ghost.js).
//
//   PK.paint.wipe(L, rng, path, o) -> 1 | 0     one pull on the layer.  L = PK.kit.paint.  path in USER px, the direction of travel.
//     w px         width of the rag (the material entry point resolves wu, in U, before it gets here)
//     strength     0..1 how much of a WET film is removed at full pressure (0.7).  1 leaves the ground; see REMOVAL below
//     carry        how far the lifted pigment is carried and laid down again along the pull, in widths (1.5); the smear thins geometrically
//                  and is at 13 percent of its start by that distance, nothing by twice it
//     pressure     the profile along the pull: [p0, p1] (default [1, 0.3]: full at the start, lifting over the last 40 percent to 0.3 so the
//                  tail is ragged), a number (constant), or a function of t in 0..1
//     side         'left' | 'right' ('right'): the side of travel the tool's edge is on; that edge is hard, the other is torn
//     tooth        0..1 (0.5) how much the weave keeps specks: in the weave's valleys, where the film is thin enough for the weave to
//                  show, the rag cannot reach and the paint stays
//     tint         an optional colour on the rag (a chalky white or pink): laid along the whole pull as a thin dry film, amount by
//                  strength x pressure; null = a clean rag
//     under        what shows through where the film is thinned: a colour, or 'ground' / undefined = PK.kit.paper (the recorded ground)
//     streaks      1 (the rag's folds and torn edge).  0 = the planted negative of rules/planted/kit_wipe.js W2 and W4: a plain rectangle blend
//                  with two hard edges and no across-structure.  Never set in a painting
//   The model is shared with the stamp approximation (materials/wipe.js) through PK.paint.wipeModel(rng, path, o, sx): the same pull, the same
//   footprint, the same rag edge and streak field, so a scene rendered both ways puts its wipes in the same place.
